@startuml use-case
' Mini GitHub Issue Tracker, terminal version.
' Project Meeting 3. Ten use cases carried over from Meeting 2 (two renamed),
' plus eight added during the terminal migration.

left to right direction
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam usecase {
  BackgroundColor #AEDCF0
  BorderColor #333333
}
skinparam actor {
  BorderColor #333333
}

actor "User" as user
actor "GitHub Server" as github

rectangle "Mini GitHub Issue Tracker (Terminal)" {

  ' ---- Carried over from Meeting 2 ----
  usecase "Sign In with GitHub" as UC1
  usecase "View Projects"       as UC2
  usecase "Create Project"      as UC3
  usecase "View Issue"          as UC4
  usecase "Create Issue"        as UC5
  usecase "Close Issue"         as UC6
  usecase "Reopen Issue"        as UC7
  usecase "View Labels"         as UC8
  usecase "Search Issue"        as UC9
  usecase "Assign User\nto Issue" as UC10

  ' ---- Added since Meeting 2 ----
  usecase "Sign Out"            as UC11
  usecase "Create Label"        as UC12
  usecase "Assign Label\nto Issue" as UC13
  usecase "Add Comment\nto Issue"  as UC14
  usecase "Filter Issues"       as UC15
  usecase "View My GitHub\nRepositories" as UC16
  usecase "Create Local User"   as UC17
  usecase "View Assignees"      as UC18
}

user --> UC1
user --> UC2
user --> UC3
user --> UC4
user --> UC5
user --> UC6
user --> UC7
user --> UC8
user --> UC9
user --> UC10
user --> UC11
user --> UC12
user --> UC13
user --> UC14
user --> UC15
user --> UC16
user --> UC17
user --> UC18

' GitHub is a secondary actor only where the network is actually involved.
UC1  --> github
UC16 --> github

' Resuming a cached session revalidates the stored token, so sign in is
' reached both explicitly and at startup.
note bottom of UC1
  Covers first-time registration and
  returning login. db_user_upsert_github
  inserts or updates on github_id, so a
  separate Sign Up use case no longer applies.
end note

note bottom of UC10
  Renamed from "Add Project Contributor".
  Assignment is per issue, not per project.
end note

@enduml
